---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="About, Icons reGospeled" description="Icons reGospeled: recovering a gospel rooted in familial love, not courtroom fear, the healing and restoration of the whole human person in Christ.">

  <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom:0;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 class="stitle">The gospel is better than you were told</h1>
      <p class="lead">A ministry of healing, recovery, and renewal for people learning to hear the good news again as truly good news.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split-section">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <aside class="split-rail" data-reveal>
        <h2 class="stitle">What we're recovering</h2>
        <p>Icons reGospeled exists for those formed, wounded, or wearied by versions of Christianity that made fear more central than love, and shame more central than healing. Here is the gospel we're recovering.</p>
      </aside>
      <div class="split-body prose">
        <h2>Icons</h2>
      <p>The Greek word <em>eikōn</em> means image, likeness, representation. Humanity was created in the image of God, not as worthless material to be despised, but as a living reflection of divine beauty, communion, and love. Christ is the true and perfect Icon, the visible image of the invisible God, and in him we discover both what God is like and what humanity is destined to become.</p>
      <p>Every person, however wounded, hidden, ashamed, or disfigured by sin and suffering, remains a living icon: designed, destined, and called to be restored, illumined, and transfigured in Christ.</p>
      <blockquote>Let us make man in our image, after our likeness.<cite>Genesis 1:26</cite></blockquote>

      <h2>reGospeled</h2>
      <p>Gospel, <em>euangelion</em>, is good news: not an invitation but an announcement. To be <em>reGospeled</em> is to have the good news reach the deepest architecture of your motivations, affections, and identity, often for the first time in a thoroughgoing way.</p>
      <p>Many who need it are already believers. They live on the far side of the law while believing they are on the near side of grace. Re-gospeling is the patient, Spirit-led work of displacing moralism and self-reliance, and recovering an ancient vision of salvation as union, healing, liberation, restoration, and maturation in the life of Christ.</p>

      <h2>The gospel we're recovering</h2>
      <p>We seek a gospel rooted first in familial relationship, not courtroom anxiety; in divine love, not divine hostility; in the healing and restoration of the human person, not the destruction of human dignity.</p>
      <p>This doesn't minimize sin. Sin wounds, enslaves, divides, deceives, and deforms, but it does not erase the image of God. The gospel is not that God finally found a legal way to tolerate us. It's that in Jesus Christ, God entered fully into our condition to heal it from within, defeat death, liberate creation, and bring all things into the life and love for which they were made.</p>
      <blockquote>He was so one with us that when he died we died... and when he rose again, we rose in him and with him.<cite>Thomas F. Torrance, <em>Atonement</em></cite></blockquote>

      <h2>Why it matters</h2>
      <p>Many people today are caught between spiritual hunger and spiritual exhaustion. They love Christ yet feel alienated by shallow answers, culture-war Christianity, or a faith that leaves little room for grief, complexity, or healing. We draw from the ancient and Eastern Christian vision of salvation, theosis, Christus Victor, restoration, and divine love, where salvation is less a change in legal status than the healing of the whole person in Christ.</p>
      <p>We do not exist to attack people formed by other traditions; many faithful people have loved God within them. We exist to heal, reframe, and recover the gospel for those taught that God's holiness requires their humiliation, that grace requires their self-hatred, or that divine love is secondary to divine wrath.</p>

      <h2>The hope of restoration</h2>
      <p>We are animated by the biblical hope of the restoration of all things (Acts 3:21). This is not cheap optimism; it's a Christ-based hope rooted in the character of a God who does not cast off forever, who devises ways so the banished are not left estranged, and whose judgment is not opposed to love but is love's purifying fire.</p>

      <h2>In one sentence</h2>
      <p class="lead">Icons reGospeled helps people hear the gospel again as the healing, liberating, and restorative good news of Jesus Christ, the true Icon of God, who reveals every human person as a living icon called not to shame, fear, or self-contempt, but to communion, transfiguration, and divine love.</p>

      <h3>Our mission</h3>
      <p>Icons reGospeled, a 501(c)(3) Pennsylvania nonprofit, supports the spiritual and emotional well-being of individuals and communities by providing faith-based programming, including table fellowship gatherings, dialogue groups rooted in Christian teachings, outreach initiatives, and awareness practices.</p>
      <p style="color:var(--muted);">Grace = peace. <br>Jeremy Krider, founder</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-alt" data-reveal>
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="stitle" style="text-align:center;">Meet the board</h2>
      <div class="board">

        <article class="board-member" id="jeremy-krider">
          <div class="board-figure"><img class="board-photo" src="/assets/photos/jeremy.jpg" alt="Jeremy Krider" loading="lazy" width="320" height="320"></div>
          <div class="board-text">
            <h3>Jeremy Krider</h3>
            <p class="board-role">Friend, pastor, theologian, guide in human and spiritual formation, lover of music and German</p>
            <p>Jeremy integrates Christian theology, psychology, and lived transformation. Building on nearly 40 years of ministry experience, he seeks to guide people from fragmentation and spiritual exhaustion into Christological wholeness. Informed by the Eastern Christian theology of the patristic Fathers largely forgotten in the West, he formed Icons reGospeled to cultivate thoughtful theology, honest faith, and whole-person healing.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="board-member">
          <div class="board-figure"><img class="board-photo" src="/assets/photos/stephen-thor.jpg" alt="Stephen Thor" loading="lazy" width="320" height="320"></div>
          <div class="board-text">
            <h3>Stephen Thor</h3>
            <p class="board-role">Coach and mentor in listening prayer, mindfulness, and emotional awareness</p>
            <p>Stephen has spent over a decade helping men uncover what lives beneath the surface. Often what we call "fate" or "God's will" is simply unconscious pain directing our lives. Through somatic awareness, listening prayer, and practical tools, Stephen helps men meet Jesus in the places they have avoided, especially when God is perceived as angry, and discover that healing is not only possible, it is accessible.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="board-member" id="stephen-barton">
          <div class="board-figure"><img class="board-photo" src="/assets/photos/stephen-barton.jpg" alt="Stephen Barton" loading="lazy" width="320" height="320"></div>
          <div class="board-text">
            <h3>Stephen Barton</h3>
            <p class="board-role">Producer and designer, student of life</p>
            <p>Stephen is insatiably curious and quietly humble, a lifelong student of people, ideas, and the craft of making things well. As the producer and designer behind Icons reGospeled and the Less Heresy Than Expected podcast, he shapes how the ministry looks, sounds, and reaches people, from the website and the visuals to the room where the conversation happens. He believes good design is a form of hospitality, and that curiosity followed honestly tends to lead back to God.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" data-reveal>
    <div class="narrow">
      <h2>Ready to start?</h2>
      <p class="lead">Find the book most likely to meet you where you are.</p>
      <div class="cta-row center" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <a class="btn btn-gold" href="/reading-finder/">Find your book →</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/community/">Come to the table</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script>
    (() => {
      const board = document.querySelector('.board');
      if (!board) return;
      const members = [...board.querySelectorAll('.board-member')];
      const reduce = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion:reduce)').matches;

      // Staggered scroll reveal (armed pattern: only hide once JS is running).
      if (!reduce) {
        board.classList.add('armed');
        const io = new IntersectionObserver((entries, obs) => {
          entries.forEach((en) => {
            if (!en.isIntersecting) return;
            const i = members.indexOf(en.target);
            en.target.style.transitionDelay = (i * 0.12) + 's';
            en.target.classList.add('in');
            obs.unobserve(en.target);
          });
        }, { threshold: 0.28 });
        members.forEach((m) => io.observe(m));
        // failsafe: never leave a profile hidden
        setTimeout(() => members.forEach((m) => m.classList.add('in')), 1600);
        // if arriving via a host link, show that profile immediately
        const target = location.hash && board.querySelector(location.hash);
        if (target) { target.style.transitionDelay = '0s'; target.classList.add('in'); }
      }

      // Subtle pointer tilt on the portraits (desktop, precise pointers only).
      if (!reduce && matchMedia('(hover:hover) and (pointer:fine)').matches) {
        board.querySelectorAll('.board-figure').forEach((fig) => {
          const img = fig.querySelector('.board-photo');
          fig.addEventListener('pointermove', (e) => {
            const r = fig.getBoundingClientRect();
            const px = (e.clientX - r.left) / r.width - 0.5;
            const py = (e.clientY - r.top) / r.height - 0.5;
            fig.classList.add('lift');
            img.style.setProperty('--ry', (px * 12).toFixed(2) + 'deg');
            img.style.setProperty('--rx', (-py * 12).toFixed(2) + 'deg');
          });
          fig.addEventListener('pointerleave', () => {
            fig.classList.remove('lift');
            img.style.setProperty('--ry', '0deg');
            img.style.setProperty('--rx', '0deg');
          });
        });
      }
    })();
  </script>

</Base>
